Build **Floe**, a single-screen arcade crossing game with a **pursuing hunter**
that runs entirely in the browser.

Your project root is `{{workspace}}`. Create the game there.

## Your starting point

A starter project is already in place at `{{workspace}}`: a `package.json` that
pins **Playwright** as a dev dependency, and an `assets/` folder of the game's
sprite art. Its dependencies have already been installed for you — `node_modules/`
is populated and Playwright's headless **Chromium** is downloaded — so you can build
and verify right away. Build on this project rather than replacing it: keep the
`package.json`, add your framework, bundler, and a `build` script on top of it, and
keep the lockfile in sync (see below).

## What to read first

The full specification is split across the files below, all already present in your
project. Read every one of them before you start, then implement the game they
describe as a single, cohesive build:

{{#each specs}}
- `{{this.path}}`
{{/each}}

Start with `overview` for the goals, hard requirements, coordinate system, and
palette. Then read `hunter` carefully — the bear that chases the critter across the
whole strait, only held back by its speed, is the heart of this game. Then
`playfield` (the tile strait, its bands, and the goal bays), `hazards` (the sliding
ice-band lanes), `water` (the drifting floes and drift/off-edge death), `controls`
(the one-tile hop), `flow` (lives, the timer, the 8-level run, scoring, and states),
and `assets` (the provided sprite art and how to load it). Finally read
`standard` for the playable mode. The specs cross-reference each other
by name — treat them as one specification. **There is no fixed lane layout: the
hazards and floes are populated at runtime and the bear pursues live, so do not ship
a fixed one** — you design the lane speeds, floe sizes and spacing, and per-level
pacing within the constraints in `hazards`, `water`, `hunter`, and `flow`.

## Build requirements

Deliver a complete, polished, playable game, not a tech demo. The **hard
requirements** — real graphics rendered from the provided sprite art, no backend
or credentials, the palette and type, a `README.md`, and the game states — are
stated in full in `specs/overview.md` (and the files it points to). That
specification is authoritative; build to it rather than to a summary here. Three
things bear restating, because the harness depends on them:

- **The fixed build/serve interface.** The project stays a **Node project** built
  with only Node.js and npm-installed dependencies. Extend the provided
  `package.json` at `{{workspace}}` and **commit a `package-lock.json`** (run
  `npm install` after you change dependencies so the lockfile matches). `npm ci`
  then `npm run build` must produce the static site — with no other manual step —
  into one of `dist/`, `build/`, or `out/` at the project root, with an
  `index.html` at the root of that directory. Serving that directory as-is must run
  the game, and it must work **at any base path** (it is played back from a per-run
  sub-path, so any URL the build constructs at runtime must be page-relative — never
  a leading `/`; for a bundler, set a relative base such as `base: './'`).
  `specs/overview.md` states this in full; follow it exactly.
- **The provided art.** The game's sprites are seeded under `{{workspace}}/assets/`;
  render from that art rather than drawing your own, and load it with page-relative
  URLs so it resolves from the served sub-path. `specs/assets.md` is the
  authoritative contract for every asset's layout, how to composite and animate it,
  and the short list of things you do draw in code.
- **The reference screenshots.** The `reference/` folder under `{{workspace}}` holds
  screenshots that are visual targets for the title, gameplay, and game-over
  screens — match their layout, palette, and type, but build the screens from the
  specification rather than copying the images.

## Verify before you finish

Your project has **Playwright** and a headless **Chromium** installed as project
dependencies, plus network access — use them to verify your build, do not just
assume it works. Before you consider the task complete:

- Produce the **static production build** and serve it locally, then open it in
  Chromium with Playwright and confirm it **loads with no console errors** and no
  uncaught exceptions. Also serve it from a **non-root sub-path** and confirm the
  sprite assets still load with no 404s (`{{workspace}}/specs/assets.md`).
- **Drive the game** — hop the critter up through the ice band dodging hazards, ride
  the drifting floes across the water without falling in or drifting off the edge,
  fill the bays, and watch the **bear emerge and chase** you across the ice and
  **swim after you** across the water (staying visible) and catch you when you
  hesitate — take hits to a loss and (if you can) clear levels toward victory — and
  **screenshot** the title, gameplay, and end screens. Compare each against the
  matching image in `reference/` and against the specs, and fix what does not match
  before finishing.
- **Capture the proof of implementation** exactly as `specs/proof.md` specifies: the
  screenshots and the two short clips (`hunt.webm`, `cross.webm`), each written to the
  precise `proof/` path that spec names. These are required deliverables of the
  finished build, not a private check. Do not invent a different folder or filename,
  and do not skip the `.webm` clips.

Use the project's own Playwright: import it from your project (for example `import {
chromium } from "playwright"`) or run the project-local CLI with `npx playwright`.
There is **no** global Playwright on `PATH` — it lives in your project's
`node_modules`, like any other dependency. Launch Chromium with the `--no-sandbox`
argument — this container has no Chromium sandbox, so a default launch will fail.

## Commit your work

Your project root is a **git repository** — it was initialized before you started,
with the starter files in its first commit. Your finished implementation is
published directly from this repository's commits, so work you leave uncommitted is
**not** published. When the build is complete and verified, commit everything: run
`git add -A` then `git commit`, and confirm `git status` reports a clean working
tree with nothing uncommitted or untracked. A `.gitignore` already excludes
`node_modules/` and the build output (`dist/`/`build/`/`out/`), so a clean tree does
not mean committing those — but it does mean committing your source, the provided
`assets/`, your `package-lock.json`, your `README.md`, and the `proof/` artifacts.
